### SCENARIO 4 — the loop a coding agent actually runs, and the one this framework is built for.
###
### Submit a payload with several distinct problems. Read the `violations` array. Change EXACTLY the
### fields it names, in exactly the way each `fixSuggestion` prescribes. Resubmit. Succeed.
###
### It fails if the violations are incomplete (the corrected payload is refused again, for something
### that was wrong the first time and went unreported), mis-pointed (the correction lands on the wrong
### field), or insufficient (fixing what they name is not enough). The test script asserts the
### strongest form of that: the set of keys that differ between the two request bodies is EQUAL to the
### set of pointers the refusal named — no more, no fewer. So "I corrected it" cannot be satisfied by
### quietly changing something the refusal never mentioned.
###
### The eight problems, and the fix each violation prescribes:
###   /reference      format               -> spell it in the declared work-order-ref shape
###   /title          max-length           -> shorten to at most 120 characters
###   /status         enum-value           -> use one of the declared values
###   /priority       required             -> supply it
###   /quoted_price   scale                -> round to 2 decimal places
###   /contact_email  format               -> send an email address
###   /external_ref   read-only-field      -> remove the field from the body
###   /customer_id    unresolved-reference -> reference a row that exists and you can read

# @name Rejected
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [422]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "reference": "job-8300",
  "title": "Replace the compressor and recommission the plant room, including the secondary circuit and the two pressure vessels on the roof",
  "status": "open",
  "quoted_price": 99.999,
  "contact_email": "site at example",
  "external_ref": "CRM-99",
  "description": "Raised by the site manager on Friday.",
  "access_code": "FIXLOOP",
  "customer_id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-00000000abcd",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### The correction. Every field the refusal named is changed as its fixSuggestion prescribes; every
### field it did not name is byte-for-byte what it was.
# @name Accepted
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "reference": "WO-8300",
  "title": "Replace the compressor and recommission the plant room",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 2,
  "quoted_price": 100.00,
  "contact_email": "site@example.test",
  "description": "Raised by the site manager on Friday.",
  "access_code": "FIXLOOP",
  "customer_id": "{{CustomerAcmeId}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### And the row really is there, carrying the untouched fields exactly as the first attempt sent them
# @name ReadTheCorrectedRow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}{{Accepted.response.headers.Location}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### The rejected attempt wrote nothing: the reference it tried to use names no row
# @name RejectedWroteNothing
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?reference=in.(job-8300,WO-8300)&limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
